🔹 Step 2: Create an IAM Policy
Let’s say you’re using DynamoDB.

Create a policy:

{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": [
        "dynamodb:GetItem",
        "dynamodb:PutItem",
        "dynamodb:UpdateItem"
      ],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:dynamodb:ap-south-1:123456789012:table/CustomerData"
    }
  ]
}
👉 This policy allows read/write access only to the CustomerData table.

🔹 Step 3: Create an IAM Role for EC2
Go to IAM > Roles > Create Role.

Choose Trusted Entity = AWS Service.

Select EC2 as the service.

Attach the above Policy to the Role.

Name it: EC2DynamoDBAccessRole.

🔹 Step 4: Attach Role to EC2 Instance
When launching the EC2 instance, under IAM Role, select EC2DynamoDBAccessRole.

If the EC2 is already running → Go to EC2 > Actions > Security > Modify IAM Role and attach the role.

🔹 Step 5: Update Application Code
Inside the EC2 instance, your application can now access DynamoDB without credentials.

The AWS SDK automatically fetches temporary credentials from the EC2 metadata service.

