Example in Java (Spring Boot)
import software.amazon.awssdk.auth.credentials.DefaultCredentialsProvider;
import software.amazon.awssdk.regions.Region;
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.s3.S3Client;
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.s3.model.GetObjectRequest;
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.s3.model.GetObjectResponse;
import java.nio.file.Paths;

public class S3ImageFetcher {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        S3Client s3 = S3Client.builder()
                .region(Region.AP_SOUTH_1)
                .credentialsProvider(DefaultCredentialsProvider.create()) // uses EC2 Role automatically
                .build();

        GetObjectRequest getObjectRequest = GetObjectRequest.builder()
                .bucket("my-images-bucket")
                .key("images/sample.jpg")  // path to your image in bucket
                .build();

        s3.getObject(getObjectRequest, Paths.get("/tmp/sample.jpg"));
        System.out.println("Image downloaded successfully!");
    }
}
Explanation:

DefaultCredentialsProvider automatically fetches credentials from the EC2 Role.

No hardcoded AWS access key/secret key is needed.

You can now serve this image in your web app.

🔹 Step 5: Optional – Restrict Policy Further
Limit access to specific prefixes in S3, e.g., only images/ folder.

Avoid giving s3:* permissions → follow least privilege principle.

